        Coordinating research projects, maritime navigation, aviation operations, and international communications between Greenwich Mean Time (GMT) regions and the Hawaii-Aleutian time zone demands precise timezone calculations. When converting from <strong>Greenwich Mean Time (GMT)</strong> to <strong>Hawaii-Aleutian Daylight Time (HDT)</strong>, the foundational rule is that HDT operates 9 hours behind the Prime Meridian baseline.
      </p>
      <p>
        Greenwich Mean Time represents the zero-degree longitudinal reference point (UTC+0). Hawaii-Aleutian Daylight Time is defined at <strong>UTC−9</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Whether you are scheduling calls with research stations in Adak or Atka, tracking flight routes across the North Pacific, or configuring server backups, using a date-aware converter ensures previous-day calendar shifts are handled accurately.
