        style="width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--line);"
      />
    </div>

    <!-- How to Convert GMT to HDT -->
    <section class="content-section" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
      <h2>How to Convert GMT to HDT</h2>
      <p>
        Converting Greenwich Mean Time to Hawaii-Aleutian Daylight Time manually is straightforward when following a 7-step procedure:
      </p>
      <ol style="padding-left: 1.2rem; line-height: 1.9;">
        <li><strong>Start with the GMT date and time:</strong> Identify the clock time in GMT (e.g., 14:30 GMT).</li>
        <li><strong>Verify the GMT baseline:</strong> Confirm that the baseline uses UTC+0.</li>
        <li><strong>Identify HDT's offset:</strong> Note that Hawaii-Aleutian Daylight Time operates at UTC−9.</li>
        <li><strong>Subtract nine hours:</strong> Subtract 9 from the GMT hour figure (14:30 minus 9 hours = 05:30).</li>
        <li><strong>Check whether the result falls below 00:00:</strong> If your calculated hour is negative, add 24 hours to the total.</li>
        <li><strong>Adjust the calendar date if needed:</strong> If the result went below 00:00 midnight, roll back the calendar day, month, or year by one.</li>
        <li><strong>Confirm the final HDT time:</strong> Record the resulting hour, minute, date, and HDT label (e.g., 05:30 HDT).</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        For instance, converting <strong>14:30 GMT</strong> yields <strong>05:30 HDT</strong> (5:30 AM HDT) on the exact same date. However, converting <strong>04:30 GMT</strong> yields 04:30 minus 9 hours = −04:30. Adding 24 hours results in <strong>19:30 HDT (7:30 PM HDT) on the previous calendar day</strong>.
      </p>
    </section>

